# Line endings in this component are not a matter of taste, and this file exists
# because two of its disciplines break without them.
#
# Three artefacts here are generated and then compared byte for byte against what
# the generator would write again - CODE-ASSURANCE.md, HUMAN_REVIEW.md and
# assurance.manifest.json - and rule J5 fails when they differ. The generators
# emit LF. On a checkout that converts to CRLF the comparison fails on every line
# while the content is identical, and the failure reports "differs in length
# only", which is a true sentence nobody can act on. Five tests fail this way on a
# stock Windows clone, before anyone has edited anything.
#
# And the malformed-input corpus is eighty-seven artifacts pinned by SHA-256. A
# single byte of end-of-line conversion invalidates a hash and takes VM-2's whole
# boundary evidence with it. Those files must never be converted by anything, and
# leaving that to a heuristic is not a bound.
#
# Everything tracked here is already LF in the index, so this file pins what is
# true today rather than changing it. Adding it renormalises nothing.

# Text is stored and checked out LF, on every platform, whatever a contributor's
# core.autocrlf says. eol=lf is the half that matters: text=auto alone would still
# hand a Windows checkout CRLF.
*                         text=auto eol=lf

# C# is text even where git's heuristic says otherwise. samples/.../Program.cs
# embeds a "NOPE\x01\x00\x00"u8 payload literal, so it carries real NUL bytes and
# is detected as binary - which means it neither diffs as source nor gets the
# guarantee above. Declaring the type removes the guess.
*.cs                      text eol=lf
*.csproj                  text eol=lf
*.slnx                    text eol=lf
*.props                   text eol=lf
*.targets                 text eol=lf
*.json                    text eol=lf
*.md                      text eol=lf
*.yml                     text eol=lf
*.txt                     text eol=lf
*.log                     text eol=lf
*.witness                 text eol=lf
*.py                      text eol=lf
*.config                  text eol=lf

# Byte-pinned evidence. Declared binary so no future heuristic, filter or
# contributor setting can rewrite a corpus artifact and break its recorded hash.
src/tests/corpus/**/*.bin binary

# The JavaScript profile's corpus is pinned the same way and was covered by nothing: the
# rule above names .bin, which is the core's extension, and the profile's artifacts are
# .bjsb. Every one of them happens to carry a NUL today, so git's heuristic calls them
# binary and no hash has moved - which is the same accident this file's own comment says is
# not a bound. Declared at JS-3a, which added nine more of them.
src/tests/corpus/**/*.bjsb binary

# The conformance suite's raw fixtures are artifact bytes pinned by the suite digest, and the
# rule above reaches none of them: it names src/tests/corpus/, and these live beside the tests
# that describe them. The digest is what makes a pin verifiable, so one byte of end-of-line
# conversion would move the revision on one platform and make every run there report
# MissingSuiteRevision - a true sentence about a suite nobody edited. Declared at JS-3a, for
# the same reason the line above was: git's heuristic calls them binary today by accident.
src/tests/conformance/**/*.bjsb binary

# THE END-USER HOST'S ENCODING FIXTURES ARE BYTES AND NOTHING ELSE, and unlike everything above
# they are .js files, so `text=auto` would take them for source and hand them to end-of-line
# conversion. One of them opens with a UTF-8 byte-order mark and the other carries a lone
# continuation byte that no encoding can make valid - and the whole of what each asserts is what
# this host does with those exact bytes. Git's NUL-byte heuristic sees neither, so the declaration
# has to be explicit. Declared at JS-3b with the host itself.
src/tests/cli/encoding/*.js binary

# THE ARCHIVED LANGUAGE SPECIFICATION IS THE ONE FILE HERE WHOSE HASH IS PUBLISHED IN A PUBLIC
# API. `JavaScriptLanguageEdition.DocumentDigest` is a public constant, it appears in the
# profile's API baseline, and every conformance report carries it - so a byte of end-of-line
# conversion would not merely break a check, it would make a published constant describe a file
# nobody has. The document is HTML and carries no NUL, so git's heuristic would take it for text
# and convert it on a checkout configured to; there is nothing accidental holding this one.
# Declared at JS-3a in the change that archived it.
src/Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript/docs/specification/*.html binary

# THE ARCHIVED CONFORMANCE SUITE, for the same reason and one more. Its SHA-256 is the
# `archive-sha256` of the retained pin and rule N15 hashes the file against it, so a byte of
# conversion breaks a check rather than passing quietly - and gzip carries no NUL guarantee either
# way, so nothing accidental is holding it. Declared at JS-3a in the change that archived it.
src/tests/conformance/pins/*.tar.gz binary

# THE RETAINED OCTANE ARCHIVE, and the same reason. It is `git archive` output at an exact commit
# with `gzip -n`, so its bytes are a function of the commit and of nothing else; a filter that
# rewrote a byte of it would move a digest the pin states and no reader could tell whether the
# workload or the checkout had changed.
src/tests/octane/pins/*.tar.gz binary

*.png                     binary
